          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CompLF+v1"} rules of CompLF
            have been designed so that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+rules"} structural rules
            (e.g. weakening and substitution) are admissible, and the remaining rules can be expressed as higher-order abstract syntax (HOAS) axioms in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+frameworks"} logical frameworks
            like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LF"} LF
            or
            = succeed "/Pure." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isabelle"} Isabelle
          %p
            First, the HOAS formulation highlights a connection between dependent type systems and partial logic. The connection is there for all dependent type systems formulated as deductive systems with judgments on raw terms (untyped
            %span.newWikiWord
              abstract syntax
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/abstract+syntax"} ?
            trees), but Nuprl-like systems make more use of the connection than most.
          %p
            Second, although the rules are not algorithmic, there is implicit propagation of type validity facts in the semantics, which is analogous to the type propagation of a dependent type checker. The primitive typing judgment form (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊩
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \Vdash A
            ) outputs validity of the type (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\,type
            ). But using the inversion rules, it’s possible to derive rules for a defined typing judgment form that inputs type validity. This will hopefully be useful for verifying a bidirectional proof checker for CompLF and similar systems.
          %h2#secondorder_formulation_of_rules Second-order formulation of rules
          %p
            This section repeats some of the rules of v1 in HOAS style, since variables and substitution (of terms) are technically expressed differently. For convenience, here is the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Nat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Nat
            elimination rule from
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CompLF+v1"} v1
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "left", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi n
                          %mo ⊩
                          %mi Nat
                          %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi Nat
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi C
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mi type
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi c
                          %mo ⊩
                          %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi zro
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi Nat
                          %mo ,
                          %mi h
                          %mo :
                          %mi C
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi c
                          %mo ⊩
                          %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi suc
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi c
                      %mo ⊩
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\begin{array}{l}
                  \\Gamma \vdash n \Vdash Nat \qquad
                  \\Gamma,x:Nat \vdash C\,type \\
                  \\Gamma \vdash c \Vdash C[zro/x] \\
                  \\Gamma,x:Nat,h:C \vdash c \Vdash C[suc(x)/x]
                  \\end{array}}
                  {\Gamma \vdash c \Vdash C[n/x]}
          %p And here it is in a traditional HOAS form:
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi natElim
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo ⊩
                  %mi Nat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊩
                  %mi Nat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi type
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  natElim\,:\,\forall n,c,C.(n \Vdash Nat) \Rightarrow
                  (\forall x.(x \Vdash Nat) \Rightarrow (C(x)\,type)) \Rightarrow
            %br/
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ⊩
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi zro
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi h
                  %mo .
